[[File:Chopping.gif|thumb|This diagram shows the full cycle of chopping. It first points directly at the object in focus. It then tilts in one direction and then the same distance in the opposite direction.]]

'''Chopping''' is a technique used with [[infrared]] telescopes to minimize interference from external or internal heat.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gemini.edu/sciops/instruments/mir/MIRChopNod.html|website=www.gemini.edu|title=Chopping and Nodding|first=Rachel|last=Mason|date=22 August 2006}}</ref> The chopper is the mechanism that rocks a [[mirror]] (typically a [[secondary mirror]]) inside a telescope back and forth in rapid succession because background radiation would change too quickly to measure otherwise.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www2.keck.hawaii.edu/inst/tools/chp/Chopping.html|website=www2.keck.hawaii.edu|first=Bob|last=Goodrich|date=12 November 1998|title=How does a Chopper Work}}</ref>

