'''Acadian literature''' is literature produced in Acadia,<ref group="Note">Acadia roughly encompasses the north and east of the Canadian province of [[New Brunswick]], as well as more isolated communities in [[Prince Edward Island]], Newfoundland and Labrador, and [[Nova Scotia]]. More broadly, Acadia also refers to the communities of the [[Acadian diaspora]] in [[Quebec]] and the United States; people of Acadian descent can also be found in France, the [[Falkland Islands]] and the [[Caribbean]]. Acadia is not officially recognized, but would constitute a nation through its language, culture, institutions, and symbols.</ref> or considered as such.

The colonial period was characterized by a scarcity of textual production, largely attributable to the challenging socio-economic circumstances prevailing at the time. The poem ''[[Evangeline]]'' by American [[Henry Wadsworth Longfellow|Henry Longfellow]] exerted a considerable influence on the emergence of Acadian literature. From the mid-19th century onwards, priest-writers began to publish. Historians such as [[Placide Gaudet]] and [[Pascal Poirier]] subsequently contributed to the field, and nationalist debates shaped the literature until the 1960s. From 1966 onwards, a new generation of authors challenged traditional values. In the early 1970s, poetry collections such as ''Cri de terre'' by [[Raymond Guy LeBlanc]], ''Acadie Rock'' by Guy Arsenault, ''Saison antérieures'' by [[Léonard Forest]], and ''Mourir à Scoudouc'' by [[Herménégilde Chiasson]] marked this turning point. The success of these writers influenced others, including [[Melvin Gallant]], whose collections of tales, ''Ti-Jean'', and [[Louis Haché]], whose historical novels were well received. {{Interlanguage link|Claude Le Bouthillier|lt=Claude Le Bouthillier|fr|Claude Le Bouthillier}} and {{Interlanguage link|Ulysse Landry|lt=Ulysse Landry|fr|Ulysse Landry}} also made an impact with their speculative fiction and poetry, respectively. In 1979, [[Antonine Maillet]] was awarded the esteemed [[Prix Goncourt]] for her novel {{Interlanguage link|Pélagie-la-Charrette|lt=''Pélagie-la-Charrette''|fr|Pélagie-la-Charrette}}.

{{Interlanguage link|Dyane Léger|lt=Dyane Léger|fr|Dyane Léger}}, the inaugural Acadian female author to be published, released a poetry collection in 1980. [[Gérald Leblanc]] also made a significant contribution during this decade.

The establishment of {{Interlanguage link|Éditions d'Acadie|lt=Éditions d'Acadie|fr|Éditions d'Acadie}} in 1972 facilitated the broader dissemination of works and enabled local publishing. Among other publishing houses, [[Perce-Neige Publishing|Éditions Perce-Neige]] has distinguished itself in the field of poetry, while Bouton d'or Acadie has established a reputation for excellence in children's literature. In 2000, Éditions d'Acadie filed for bankruptcy, compelling numerous authors to seek out publishers in Quebec or Ontario. A portion of the repertoire remains unavailable in bookstores. Several anthologies and literary histories have been published, notably by {{Interlanguage link|Marguerite Maillet|lt=Marguerite Maillet|fr|Marguerite Maillet}} and {{Interlanguage link|David Lonergan|lt=David Lonergan|fr|David Lonergan}}. Criticism only truly developed in the 1990s, yet Acadian authors faced challenges in gaining recognition and popularizing their works outside of Acadia. However, the number of writers and the quality of their work have grown significantly.

