'''Malika Agueznay''' (born 1934 in [[Marrakesh]], Morocco)<ref>{{Cite book |last=Cheurfi |first=Achour |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_n0PAQAAMAAJ&q=Malika+Agueznay |title=L'encyclopédie maghrébine |date=2007 |publisher=Casbah éditions |isbn=978-9961-64-641-0 |language=fr}}</ref> is a Moroccan visual artist. For her contributions to modern art and abstraction in the history of Moroccan visual art, she became internationally known as Morocco’s first woman [[Modern art|Modernist artist]] and member of the experimental [[School of Fine Arts of Casablanca|Casablanca Art School]].

{{Short description|Subfield}}
[[File:NFDI4BIOIMAGE Calendar Cover 2025.png|thumb|194x194px|Integration of data from a [[confocal microscopy]] image and spatial transcriptomics data on human [[medulloblastoma]].]]'''Spatial biology''' is the field of [[biology]] that visualizes the spatial organization of [[Biomolecule|biomolecules]] in [[Tissue (biology)|tissues]].<ref name=":1" />The term "spatial biology" is mostly used in the context of [[High throughput biology|high-throughput techniques]] that detect distribution of biomolecules, a field also called '''spatial [[omics]]'''.<ref name=":2">{{cite web |last1=Bruns |first1=Volker |title=Spatial Biology 101 |url=https://www.smartsensing.blog/spatial-biology-101 |website=Smart Sensing insights blog |publisher=Fraunhofer IIS |access-date=2026-04-10}}</ref>

Spatial biology encompasses different levels of cellular resolution including (1) [[subcellular localization]] of [[DNA]], [[RNA]], and [[Protein|proteins]], (2) [[Single-cell analysis|single-cell]] resolution and ''[[in situ]]'' communications like [[cell-cell interactions]] and [[cell signaling]], (3) cellular neighborhoods, regions, or [[Microenvironment (biology)|microenvironments]], and (4) [[Tissue (biology)|tissue]] architecture and organization in [[Organ (biology)|organs]]. Dysregulation of tissue organization is a common feature in human disease progression including [[tumorigenesis]] and [[Neurodegenerative disease|neurodegeneration]].<ref name=":2" />

