{{Short description|Genre of literature involving espionage}}
{{Distinguish|text=[[Espionage fiction]] or [[Spy fiction]]}}

{{Literature}}{{Intelligence}}
'''Intelligence literature''', sometimes referred to as '''espionage nonfiction''', is a genre of [[non-fiction]] or [[History|historical]] [[literature]], written in any language, that focuses on the [[intelligence field]], and its most popular subfield known as [[espionage]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kent |first=Sherman |date=September 22, 1993 |title=The Need for an Intelligence Literature |url=https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/Need-for-Intelligence-Literature.pdf |website=CIA Reading Room}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Wheeler |first=Douglas L. |date=2015 |title=The Literature of Intelligence: 'Another Kind of Need to Know.' |url=https://www.afio.com/publications/WHEELER_Douglas_Guide_to_The_Literature_of_Intelligence_from_AFIO_INTEL_WINTER2014-15_Vol21_No1.pdf |website=Association of Former Intelligence Officers}}</ref> This field of literature includes [[Biography|biographies]] and [[Autobiography|autobiographies]] of [[intelligence officer]]s, historical [[research]] and [[analysis]] of intelligence [[Clandestine operation|operations]] and [[Military operation|missions]], studies of [[undercover]] work, [[Jurisprudence|policy and legal studies]] surrounding the fields of [[intelligence law]], [[intelligence history]], and [[national security]] law, [[Academic journal|academic]] and [[Professional magazine|professional journals]], [[essay]]s, [[textbook]]s, and more.<ref>{{Cite web |title=[FOREWORD THE MEASURE OF MATURITY IN ANY PROFESSION IS THE LITERATURE OF THAT PROFESSION.] {{!}} CIA FOIA (foia.cia.gov) |url=https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/document/cia-rdp80b01676r004300180038-7 |access-date=2025-02-28 |website=www.cia.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hunter |first=David H. |date=1978-10-01 |title=The Evolution of Literature on United States Intelligence |url=https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0095327x7800500102 |journal=Armed Forces & Society |language=en |volume=5 |issue=1 |pages=31–52 |doi=10.1177/0095327X7800500102 |issn=0095-327X|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Other works of intelligence literature include [[Official history|official histories]], official reports, [[tradecraft]] and [[Technical communication|technical manuals]], [[Declassification|declassified documents]] and [[Archive|archival materials]], and [[Oral history|oral histories]] and [[interview]]s.<ref>{{Cite web |title=DEVELOPMENT OF INTELLIGENCE LITERATURE {{!}} CIA FOIA (foia.cia.gov) |url=https://www.cia.gov/readingroom/document/cia-rdp62-00631r000300210011-5 |access-date=2025-02-28 |website=www.cia.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Wheeler |first=Douglas L. |date=1986 |title=Secrecy, Intelligence Literature and History |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/44326244 |journal=American Intelligence Journal |volume=7 |issue=4 |pages=21–27 |jstor=44326244 |issn=0883-072X}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |date=2012 |title=INTELLIGENCE STUDIES: SOME THOUGHTS ON THE STATE OF THE ART |url=https://www.ceeol.com/search/article-detail?id=161831 |journal=Analele Universităţii din Bucureşti. Seria Ştiinţe Politice |language=English |volume=XIV |issue=1 |pages=5–17 |issn=1582-2486}}</ref>

{{Poem quote|text="The literature of intelligence is rich and voluminous... The topic of intelligence has long captured the imagination of readers, in part because it has been sensationalized and made to sound more intriguing than it probably is in real life... Authors breathlessly await the declassification of government documents decades after a particularly nasty chapter of American history or, if they cannot wait, seek to obtain documents expeditiously through the Freedom of Information Act process."|source=https://doi.org/10.1093/acrefore/9780190846626.013.308|sign=William C. Spracher|title=Teaching Intelligence in the United States, the United Kingdom, and Canada}}

